From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Mon, 21 Feb 94 16:22:34 GMT
Subject: [#94-2-197] PCR: Creation Ex Nihilo

Mike Price:
>creation ex nihilo requires solving with physics, not metaphysics.

I'll attempt a summary of our positions (may need corrections):
Talking physics, the "problem" of creation ex nihilo has virtually been
solved, and we have every confidence that it will be.  The universe of
space and time arrived from nothing by quantum tunnelling (the tunnel has
one end).  At first the universe was in the "false" vacuum state,
spacetime would appear (to a brave observer) like empty space but had a
tremendous negative pressure.  This produced a massive cosmic repulsion
with each Planck region of space doubling in size about every 10^-35
seconds.  The exponential growth has been dubbed "inflation".

During inflation some 10^69 joules of energy were created.  When a fluid
with negative pressure expands its energy is increased, like when a +ve
pressure fluid is compressed.  Then the unstable false vacuum decayed,
and inflation ceased.  The universe had turned cold, but then the energy
of the false vacuum was transferred to the universe, suddenly heating it
to about 10^27 K.  Thereafter expansion continued from the momentum of
the original explosion.

The positive mass energy of the universe has been paid for by negative
gravitational energy, so in an absolute sense TINSTAAFL, but luckily from
our viewpoint TISATAAFL.

But some people are not convinced by Big Bang theories and believe them 
to be irrational, or necessarily incomplete.  Speaking metaphysically, by
postulating the existence of additional worlds or universes beyond those
required by the MWI of QM, some loose ends are tied up.  All possible
universes, all the permissible variations of history (I'll take Mike's
advice and agree to quit using "reality perms" :-) exist in superposition,
and each of them has its opposite (with matter / antimatter reversal).  In
an absolute sense each universe is just a bunch of spacetime with all the
events locked in place by its own natural laws, but since each is
cancelled out by its opposite the result is zero.  Nothing ever popped
into existence from nothing.  Since we do not share consciousness with
our antimatter twins, existence is quite real enough to us.

The operationalist or positivist is perfectly entitled to point out that
a difference that makes no difference is no difference.  But by their own
logic the existence of these extra universes is no problem.  As everyone
agrees (except hypothetical hyperbeings) the extra universes make no
practical difference to us.  The PCRist may believe that the metaphysical
+ physical model is more philosophically satisfying, and although may be
criticised on grounds of Occam's Razor for example, the effect can only
be to reduce somewhat the confidence value / credibility / estimated
probability of the belief.  (Unless it turns out that there is some fatal
flaw to put the credibility down something similar to that of {deathism
is an extropic strategy which works to the benefit of the individual
practising it, 10^-20}.)

>> [On credibility of beliefs -- e.g. QM interpretations]       I'm
>> rather confident that the correct interpretation comes from this
>> family [Many Worlds / Many Minds or something in this class as opposed
>> to, say, Copenhagenism], as most of us on this list agree
>
>I rather doubt that most of us would agree on this, of all things...

In previous discussions on the List, most active posters have favoured
Many Worlds (together with 9 out of 10 cats that expressed a preference
:-).  I don't believe I should rely too heavily on argumentum ad numeram,
but I have more respect for the views of Extropians than those of the
masses, and Extropian thought is one factor that I take into account when
estimating the confidence value I have in a belief. 

>> In the multiverses of each human, virtually all worlds have far 
>> more matter than antimatter.  The antimatter universes are
>> inaccessible to us, outside of all of our multiverses and outside of
>> MWI's "single physical reality" (but your antimatter twin is reading
>> this at this very moment!).
>
>But as I said (and you seem to have ignored) it makes no sense to
>distinguish between preponderances of matter and antimatter in other
>universes - because you have no way of saying how they are different. 
>We cannot test for it and neither can my "antimatter twin".

These antimatter universes (in my 2nd sentence) are part of the optional
metaphysical solution, I've never claimed that they were necessary in the
physics models.  My suggestion is just that each possible (matter)
universe has its equal opposite, or all the possible universes come in
pairs.  So physics has the answers for those who are satisfied with Big
Bang models, and for those who seek a little more there is the option of
a few more universes.

(As for the 1st sentence I was reiterating your statement about many
worlds' required worlds being virtually all matter.)

Still, I do think that my universes -> multiverses -> omniverse is a
better terminology than {world = spacetime continuum} and {universe = many
spacetime continuums/universes/worlds = the single reality model of QM}.
The vision of each world being a branch of a tree almost seems to imply
that alternate worlds are in different places, although the tree is
reality space not physical space. 

Damn!  If I'm not using "reality perms", I'd better call it the Many
Universes model.  Or All Possible Universes.

What sounds more credible?:
(i) God plays dice in elementary number theory / arithmetic is random.
(ii) Math law is not necessarily the same in alternate universes, minor
differences of sufficient logical depth are insufficient to permanently
diverge consciousness, therefore an individual may occupy a multiverse of
alternates with the subjective effect that the laws appear to change at
random each time a computation (observation) is performed.
(iii) Some other explanation for Chaitin's findings.

