Subject: [#94-2-202] Animal Rights (was "rBGH-injected...")

The First Extropian Squirrel transmits his thoughts:

>    Larger-brained creatures exhibit more complex behavior.  The Squirrel
>recalls reading of an instance in which one of the human companions of
>Koko (who is a gorilla who uses American sign language) made the following
>offer: ~"Koko, if you will clean up your room, I will give you a cookie."~
>Koko accomplished the designated task and then approached her companion
>with an outstretched hand.  This instance seems to be a convincing example
>of a *contract* with a non-human species.

Some non-humans might be said to occupy an intermediate category between
"semi-rights" of irrational beasts and "full" rights of humans.  One thing
about these trained animal examples is that the contract has always been
initiated by humans.  It would have been great if the gorilla had made the
offer.  In previous discussions one well-known former list participant said
something to the effect that the day a whale offered to conduct trades with
him, he'd be convinced that we should take notice of its rights.  

Splash! "I want to go long on live cattle, 5 contracts at market.  Set
the stop 20 points from the fill."
"But how much margin have you deposited, Mr Whale?"

I recall a report of a chimp that was claimed to have an amazing 
vocabulary, but later it was discovered that the researchers hadn't been
sufficiently rigorous in their interpretation of the results.  In another
case, Lyall Watson's "Hundredth Monkey" tale about macaques was exposed
as a fraud after a few years -- I found an old edition of *The Skeptical
Inquirer* that I had lying around, which happened to have a couple of
pages on "HMPhenomenon".

Trained dogs might be considered to have entered into a contract, the
terms of which may be memorised for some time.  Recently a climber was
missing for 2 nights on the Scottish Cairngorms in sub-zero temperatures.
An alsatian rescue dog had been trained to expect to receive a prize, a
squeaky toy, in return for satisfactorily tracking down a lost person.
When the dog caught the scent it ran on to discover the climber (still
in quite good condition due to experience and preparation), located about
half a mile in advance of the human rescue forces.  The dog licked the
climber's face, wagged its tail, briefly rolled in the snow (allegedly
with delight), then ran back to claim its reward. 

