Mike Price writes
>I was intrigued by January 94's Scientific American article _The Search
>for Strange Matter_. ...
>
>Any relevance to extropians?  Perhaps not.  But I like to imagine that
>nanotech is not the end of the road.  If strangelet quark structures
>occur naturally, and perhaps even if they don't, then we have the
>possibility of one day constructing femtomachines with atomic nuclear
>densities, engineered down on the femtoscale, 10^-15m.  

There was, as I recall, a lot of discussion of this on the list in May of '93.  
I don't want to recreate my long messages on nuclear physics that I sent
out then, but I will summarize my depressing conclusion after looking into
the possiblilty of strangelet-based femtotech: nuclear matter, strange or
not, is always liquid, and is thus hard to imagine building anything out
of.  Strange matter might help with things nuclear physics can already do,
like transmutation and the conversion of matter into energy, but it doesn't
seem like you could build machines of high mechanical complexity out of it.

>At least we
>mustn't think of nanotech as the "last technological revolution" as EOC
>so boldly and entropically declares.

It's the last technological revolution that doesn't depend on unknown
physics or the mysterious capabilities of superhuman intelligences.  Which
is not, of course, to say that it is the last which will occur, just that
it's the last that we can confidently understand.  Beyond lies Singularity.


