From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Tue, 22 Feb 94 06:02:48 GMT
Subject: [#94-2-210] Wormholes and Multi-Alien Empires

Harry asks for a description of the term basement universe.  A basement
universe is an artificially created extension of our universe, 
connected to ours by a wormhole(s).  Other terms are daughter, baby and
child universes, although some of the latter terms also cover universes
which completely break away from ours.  Usage varies in the literature.

I believe the term comes from a phrase by Alan Guth (him who invented
cosmic inflation) where he speculated, in the early 1980s, that our
universe may have been created in someone's basement.  Guth was
wondering what would happen if inflation was artificially generated in
a laboratory.  His calculations said that space would expand to create
another universe.  The size of the region, and the amount of non-
gravitational energy it contains, is dependent on how long the inflation
proceeds for.  Instead of expanding out into the laboratory the
expansion is into higher dimensions, in a manner of speaking, creating
a region connected to our universe with a short-lived wormhole (they
only knew of temporary, non-traversable wormholes in those days).  

I'm assuming that the connection can be held open with a traversable
wormhole.  Thus a basement universe is a region accessible via the
wormhole.  By controlling the time-dilation of the connecting wormholes
the flow of time in a basement universe can be dilated (slowed down) or
contracted (speeded up), with respect to external time.  By nesting one
universe inside another any differential rate can be achieved, returning
the solutions to infinite computations in finite time.

Tim Freeman observes:
> 
> I wonder if public key cryptography would be practical if it were
> possible to spawn basement universes to break the cryptography?

Yes, the minus side is that public key encryption would be breakable
with basement universes.  Code-breaking number crunchers, in a speeded
up basement universe, could crack any code that relies of computational
complexity.  The plus side is that wormholes also (seem to) provide an
absolutely secure physical channel down which to communicate, so you
might not feel obliged to encrypt sensitive material anyway.  Or you
could use one-time pads, as Tim mentions.

